Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.310 align:start position:0%
 
jackie chan is beloved but he has  released as many flops to smash hits did  anyone really need to see him crap  falling his way around the world in 80  days keep watching for more of his best  and worst  not many people would disagree that  jackie chan found true mass appeal with  rush hour his notoriety had already  begun to spread into the western  hemisphere before the movie was released  in 1998 but it was this hilarious buddy  cop hit which catapulted jackie into the  upper echelons of stardom in rush hour  chum plays a hong kong detective brought  over to the los angeles area to help  track down an old nemesis in an attempt  to get him out of their hair the local  police force pairs him up with detective  carter who recently botched a vital  operation and was due for some form of  punishment there are so many delightful  elements that made this buddy cop movie  work while the plot may be a little  simplistic its straightforward formula  allows the stars to really shine shannon  is in top form at this point a veteran  of action cinema who is still young  enough to tumble through obstacles and  sail through the air the juxtaposition  of chan's regimented detectives speaking  in rigid english with tucker's lanky  fountain of jokester diatribes also  makes for a hilarious double act thank  you thank you um what the hell are you  doing  i just being polite well next time  people liked my nuts  in short rush hour is a movie that  reminds you why you love movies and  easily stands up as one of jackie chan's  best the medallion fell victim to the  same monster that many early 2000s films  did it was wooed by the overwhelming  allure of new age cgi unfortunately the  filmmakers failed to recognize who they  had on board for this film namely the  king of fight choreography and stunt  work instead they opted to replace all  his combat experience with cheesy  computer animated super powers the film  struggles to find an identity most of  the way through stumbling over awkward  attempts at humor missing entirely on  moments of sincerity and failing to  properly utilize this action star even  in jackie chan's lesser movies it's easy  to let go and have fun because he always  looks like he's having fun too but  that's not the case here the medallion  is a big choppy cgi backflip followed by  a face plant into the dirt  it was around the release of rumble in  the bronx that insurance companies were  getting nervous about ensuring jackie  chan and watching this movie will make  it pretty obvious why that was the case  chan is known for performing his own  stunts rumble and the bronx features  some of his most daring of all in this  1995 movie chum plays a hong kong cup on  vacation for his uncle's wedding in new  york things take a turn for the worst  however when the grocery store his uncle  just sold falls victim to a protection  racket from a local biker gang the hero  decides to fight back out of a misguided  sense of nobility leading him to face  off against hordes of ruthless bikers  and mobsters the movie itself is pretty  low budget but you'll rarely notice  thanks to jackie's incredible fighting  choreography and death defying stunts  the action star is in peak form here  with so much energy it practically  bubbles up out of him and onto the  screen the elaborate battle sequences  highlight his ability to use his  surroundings too as he fights back with  everything around him rumble in the  bronx is a testament to both jackie  chan's action skills and his star power  and this still holds up all these years  later  if you've ever had a fever dream  involving john cusack crossing swords  with jackie chan then prepare to have  your mind blown the sheer absurdity of  dragonblade delivers a sliver of joy but  sadly fails to rescue the movie from  being a colossal dern other than the  novelty of some of the action most of  dragonblade falls flat once again the  downfall of any jackie chan flick is its  creator's inability to utilize their  stars properly bizarrely the cast is  backed up by not just john cusack but  also adrian brodie and no part of the  script offers any suggestion as to why  they climbed on board this disaster  still the fight sequences at least put  chan's abilities on full display showing  that he's got some juice left in the  tank a few sparks of life fail to ignite  the fuse on this lackluster ride though  there's very little story to follow and  the little there is tends to be driven  by cringe-worthy cliches best to skip  out on this one  the best part of the legend of drunken  master is getting to see a fresh face  jackie chan fully embrace his strengths  while his martial arts abilities are  dazzling it's chan's animated  expressions and gift for physical comedy  that sets him apart from his  counterparts and this kung fu sequel is  chan's first film to fully lean into  this jackie plays wang feihung the son  to a local merchant who mistakenly mixes  up a prescription of ginseng root with a  fellow traveler's chinese artifact his  attempts to cover up his mistake only  result in further disaster as it turns  out however wang is adept in the art of  drunken boxing and becomes an  increasingly adept fighter the more  inebriated he becomes  do you think that's a good idea don't  worry it gives him power the legend of  drunken master is arguably one of chan's  best displays of physical comedy while  the first film was pretty fun to watch  this sequel is more fully formed indeed  there are a few other jackie chan films  that so adeptly utilize every element of  the actor's talent  the buddy cop genre is a tried and true  formula that leads straight to america's  hearts unfortunately even the greats can  make a wrong turn or two skip trace  teams jackie chan up with goofball  supreme johnny knox film you might  assume that funny plus funny would equal  hysterical but there are apparently some  diminishing returns at play here once  again jackie plays a hong kong detective  and once again he is paired up with an  apathetic american the two follow a  trail of cliches to a predictable ending  proving once and for all that anytime a  chan vehicle fails to deliver it's down  to lackluster rising because whenever  it's time for a fight you can't help but  fall in love just like most shaky chan  films you'll at least get some  entertainment value out of skip trace  but that's about all you'll get  it speaks volumes about jackie chan's  work ethic that in the same year he led  a blockbuster hit alongside chris tucker  he also wrote and directed who am i this  absolutely named film stars jackie as a  special forces operative who suffers  amnesia after his aircraft crashes in  the south african jungle after spending  weeks living among the natives and  recuperating he hitches a ride with a  cross-country rally car team to  johannesburg simultaneously embarking  upon a journey of self-discovery in who  am i chan is a merely a lovable kung fu  hero he's a character with genuine  layers filled with plenty of potential  for emotional resonance the  responsibility of directing did nothing  to slow down chan's lust for thrilling  action sequences either and to ami  feature some of the coolest action  scenes in the actor's filmography  to put it bluntly around the world in 80  days is a serious disappointment the  beloved jules verne classic was ripe for  adaptation but disney wrapped their  talons around it and took off in totally  the wrong direction rather than remain  true to the source material they  attempted to transform it into a jackie  chan movie and while there's obviously  nothing wrong with a jackie chan movie  you can't force what isn't there with a  massive budget you can at least say that  the movie does look decent but this does  nothing to disguise the fact that every  attempt at humor falls flat on its face  the endless pratfalls and terrible line  deliveries start to become genuinely  annoying after a while too in the end  the only thing around the world in 80  days really achieves is making you feel  sorry for the actors involved  i can tell when we're not wanted  by the time shanghai noon was released  in 2000 it appeared that jackie chan was  trying to find new challenges for  himself next on the list apparently was  a good old-fashioned american western in  this movie chan plays chon wang an  imperial guard from the forbidden city  in the late 19th century on a mission to  retrieve a princess who has been taken  hostage by a defector early in his  journey wang meets owen wilson's outlaw  royal bannon the two form an unlikely  alliance with o'bannon teaching wang the  ways of the west as they navigate a  xenophobic and highly dangerous  landscape shanghai noon is a great  action comedy that bears most of jackie  chan's fights with the dry owen wilson  narrating exclamations on the sidelines  this offbeat genre casting doesn't seem  like it should work but it does even  better shanghai noon doesn't take itself  too seriously at all making it all the  easier to sit back and have fun with it  the tuxedo is easily one of jackie  chan's most disappointing films the  concept seems cute on paper but is  executed so poorly that it almost has  you rooting against its lead usually  movie audiences are willing to take a  walk in fantasyland when it comes to  convenient plot devices but they're  technologically enhanced tuxedo ploy  falls flat from the moment chan slips  into his penguin suit in this movie chan  plays a limo driver charged with driving  a debonair bomb type when his passenger  is put out of commission when he puts on  the man's tuxedo however he discovers  that it enhances his combat skills and  turns him into an accidental secret  agent unfortunately that tuxedo is so  heavily overproduced that a shiny veneer  actually dulls jackie chan's raw star  power the only thing this camby flop  does is show that even after losing 90  minutes of your lives to utter  mediocrity you're probably still willing  to forgive a star as lovable as chan  the third entry in the police story  series finds jackie chan reprising his  role as its resident supercop to go  undercover and infiltrate a major drug  rink supercop marks the beginning of  jackie's transition to hollywood from  the hong kong film market  it takes all the high-flying jackie chan  panache and adds a big heaping scoop of  old-school pyrotechnics and plenty of  gunfire to boot  at times jackie chan movies can take a  careless approach to story structure  opting instead for playful banter as a  means of filling the void between action  sequences supercop avoids this flaw  however instead blending all the  necessary elements of a truly great  action flick the physical comedy  transcends the language barrier the  story is intriguing and the stunts will  make you just the right kind of nervous  hong kong action movies don't get much  more fun than this  the route for every action megastar  eventually passes through kid-friendly  territory and no plot device is more  familiar than the secret agent getting  stuck babysitting a bunch of kids  perhaps that's why the spy next door  feels so exhaustingly trite right from  the opening credits jackie plays an  ex-spy looking to settle down in  suburbia when his next door neighbor  girlfriend needs to go out of town  inevitably he offers to watch after her  three kids and hilarity ensues or at  least it should ensue not only is the  spy next door wildly unoriginal but the  jackie chan of this movie seems totally  drained of the childish joy for which he  has known and loved in fact the spy next  door falls flat in every department and  the action sequences in particular are a  grim reminder of a sad truth that jackie  chan is getting old truth is there are  much better chan movies out there this  one just isn't worth your time  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movie stars are coming  soon subscribe to our youtube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]

